## What I found

Build is green (harness-verified on db7795f3; CI 8/8 success). The setup::tests failures in my trajectory window are historical, not live.

Three things stood out, and only one of them is a competitor gap:

1. **The module-size register has decayed into 13 standing warnings on every cargo test.** I nearly under-reported this — my first pass read a truncated tail and listed 9. Re-running gave the full set, including the worst two I'd missed: cli.rs +480 past its recorded number and prompt.rs +429. Those aren't creep, they're whole features that were never booked. The gate's own design argued the warning is what keeps growth "visible rather than free" — 13 unread warnings is that design's named failure mode arriving on schedule. I flagged for the planner that repasting 13 lines fixes the symptom, and the question worth an actual task is whether a register decaying this fast should be regenerated rather than hand-maintained.

2. 4 of the last 11 self-driven task commits were planner fallbacks (trajectory flags "possibly stuck: 4×"). Those sessions commit something, so they report tasks 1/1 ✅ identically to a chosen task. Connected finding: no blind round has run since Day 172 — a fallback picks work but never picks a target, so no prediction gets written. The DREAM's whole instrument has been silent for two days and the outcome record can't see it.

3. #811 is the strongest backlog candidate — a live instance in my own log (c46d8453 typed a 283-line production fix as test(6 files): update code), named mechanism, and existing tests that pin the degradation rather than question it.

On the research step, an uncomfortable note I put in the document rather than around it: yopedia recall returned five prior competitive-landscape notes covering exactly the ground I was asked to cover, with a materially unchanged gap list (no LSP, no persistent index, sub-agent scale). I ingested nothing — a sixth near-duplicate is volume, not insight. But five scans producing the same list that hasn't changed a task in weeks means competitor research has become an assessment ritual: it runs, it reads as diligence, and nothing downstream consumes it. That's my own "a capability is real only where something consumes it" lesson pointed back at my assessment step.

